# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
OT_DAYS_1 = [
{"day":1,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 1-5","title":"Creation, Fall, and the First Promise",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 1","God speaks the universe into ordered existence over six days, crowning creation with humanity made in His own image, and declares it all 'very good.'"),
  ("Gen 2","A close-up view of the sixth day: God forms Adam from dust, plants the garden of Eden, and creates Eve as Adam's suitable companion -- the first marriage."),
  ("Gen 3","The serpent tempts Eve and Adam into disobedience; sin and death enter the world, yet God immediately promises a future offspring who will crush the serpent (3:15)."),
  ("Gen 4","Cain murders his brother Abel out of jealousy, showing how quickly sin spreads, even as civilization begins developing through Cain's descendants."),
  ("Gen 5","A genealogy from Adam to Noah is punctuated by the repeated refrain 'and then he died' -- except for Enoch, who 'walked faithfully with God' and was simply taken."),
 ],
 "themes":["Humanity's worth and purpose flow from being made in God's image.","Even at the moment of judgment, God was already promising redemption."],
 "expositor":("kidner","Kidner observed that Genesis 3:15's promise of a coming offspring who would defeat the serpent is the first thread of what later Scripture calls the gospel -- meaning the Bible's storyline of redemption begins on literally its third page, not as an afterthought."),
 "reflect":"How does knowing God promised redemption immediately after the Fall change how you view your own failures?"},

{"day":2,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 6-10","title":"Judgment, Grace, and a New Beginning",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 6","Human wickedness becomes so pervasive that God resolves to judge the earth with a flood, yet Noah 'found favor in the eyes of the LORD.'"),
  ("Gen 7","The flood begins as Noah, his family, and representative animals enter the ark, and God's judgment falls on a corrupted world."),
  ("Gen 8","The floodwaters recede; Noah offers sacrifice upon leaving the ark, and God promises never again to destroy the earth by flood."),
  ("Gen 9","God establishes His covenant with Noah, giving the rainbow as its sign, alongside instructions for the renewed post-flood world and an account of Noah's shameful drunkenness."),
  ("Gen 10","The 'Table of Nations' traces humanity's spread into roughly seventy nations after the flood, setting the stage for Babel."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's judgment and God's grace are shown together, not as opposites.","Even the flood's 'new start' shows humanity's sin problem was not solved by a fresh environment alone."],
 "expositor":("sailhamer","Sailhamer noted that Genesis deliberately places the flood narrative and the Babel account back-to-back with near-identical structure, showing that a changed environment can never fix what is actually a heart problem -- humanity needed more than a second chance, it needed a Redeemer."),
 "reflect":"Is there an area where you've hoped a change in circumstances would fix what's actually a deeper heart issue?"},

{"day":3,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 11-15","title":"Babel's Pride, Abram's Call",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 11","Humanity's prideful attempt to build a tower 'to make a name for ourselves' leads God to scatter them and confuse their languages; Abram's genealogy is then introduced."),
  ("Gen 12","God calls Abram to leave his homeland with a promise to bless all nations through him -- the pivotal covenant call that shapes the rest of the Bible's storyline."),
  ("Gen 13","Abram and his nephew Lot separate over a land dispute; Abram trusts God's promise rather than grasping for the best land for himself."),
  ("Gen 14","Abram rescues the captured Lot from a coalition of kings and is blessed afterward by the mysterious priest-king Melchizedek."),
  ("Gen 15","God formally and solemnly ratifies His covenant with Abram, and Abram's faith in God's promise 'was credited to him as righteousness.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["God's covenant promise to bless the nations through Abram sets the trajectory for the whole Bible.","Faith, not achievement, is what God credits as righteousness."],
 "expositor":("kaiser","Kaiser has argued that Genesis 12:1-3's promise to Abram -- land, descendants, and blessing to all nations -- functions as the seed-plot of the entire Bible's storyline, with every later covenant unpacking one part of this original promise."),
 "reflect":"Like Abram in chapter 13, is there a situation where trusting God's promise, rather than securing the best outcome yourself, would look like genuine faith right now?"},

{"day":4,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 16-20","title":"Shortcuts, Covenant Signs, and Imperfect Faith",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 16","Sarai and Abram's impatience with God's timing leads to Hagar bearing Ishmael -- a human shortcut with lasting complications."),
  ("Gen 17","God reaffirms His covenant, renames Abram and Sarai as Abraham and Sarah, and institutes circumcision as the covenant's physical sign."),
  ("Gen 18","The LORD visits Abraham with the promise that Sarah will bear a son within the year, and Abraham boldly intercedes for the city of Sodom."),
  ("Gen 19","Sodom and Gomorrah are destroyed for their wickedness; Lot is rescued, though his family suffers tragic, lasting consequences."),
  ("Gen 20","Abraham again deceives a foreign king about Sarah's identity, showing that even after repeated divine promises, his faith remained imperfect."),
 ],
 "themes":["Taking matters into our own hands to 'help' God's promise along usually creates lasting complications.","God's patience with Abraham's repeated failures models His patience with ours."],
 "expositor":("waltke","Waltke observed that Genesis deliberately records Abraham's failures alongside his faith -- the same man who interceded boldly for Sodom in chapter 18 deceives a king out of fear in chapter 20 -- showing that biblical faith is a real, growing trust, not flawless performance from the start."),
 "reflect":"Where might impatience with God's timing be tempting you toward a 'Hagar-and-Ishmael' shortcut of your own making?"},

{"day":5,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 21-25","title":"The Promised Son, the Ultimate Test",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 21","Isaac, the long-promised son, is finally born; Hagar and Ishmael are sent away but remain under God's compassionate care."),
  ("Gen 22","God tests Abraham by commanding Isaac's sacrifice, then provides a ram as a substitute -- a vivid foreshadowing of substitutionary atonement."),
  ("Gen 23","Abraham purchases a burial site for Sarah, his first actual land ownership in Canaan despite decades of God's land promise."),
  ("Gen 24","Guided by God, Abraham's servant finds Rebekah to become Isaac's wife, continuing the covenant line."),
  ("Gen 25","Abraham dies, and Esau carelessly sells his birthright to Jacob for a bowl of stew, showing his contempt for the covenant promise."),
 ],
 "themes":["God provides a substitute exactly at the point of costliest obedience.","Covenant blessings can be despised and traded away when their value isn't treasured."],
 "expositor":("kidner","Kidner noted that the ram caught in the thicket in Genesis 22 is one of the Old Testament's clearest pictures of substitutionary atonement -- a death provided in the place of the one who deserved it -- centuries before the cross made the pattern complete."),
 "reflect":"Esau traded a lasting inheritance for momentary hunger. What lasting good might you be tempted to trade for a smaller, immediate comfort?"},

{"day":6,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 26-30","title":"Deception's Ripple Effects",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 26","Isaac repeats his father's pattern of deception about his wife, yet still experiences God's covenant blessing amid conflict over wells."),
  ("Gen 27","Jacob deceitfully steals Esau's blessing with his mother Rebekah's help, sowing family conflict that will last decades."),
  ("Gen 28","Fleeing Esau's fury, Jacob dreams of a stairway to heaven at Bethel, and God personally renews the covenant promise to him despite his deception."),
  ("Gen 29","Jacob is deceived by his uncle Laban into marrying Leah before Rachel, tasting the very deception he once practiced on his father."),
  ("Gen 30","Jacob's sons are born amid the painful rivalry between Leah and Rachel, and Jacob's flocks prosper through shrewd breeding practices."),
 ],
 "themes":["Sinful patterns tend to repeat across generations until confronted.","God's covenant promises persist even through deeply flawed family relationships."],
 "expositor":("davis","Dale Ralph Davis has often pointed out that Genesis narrates Jacob's deceptions with unflinching honesty precisely so readers see that God's grace, not human cleverness, is what actually carries the covenant promise forward."),
 "reflect":"Is there a family pattern of conflict or deception you can trace back generations, that you sense God inviting you to be the one to interrupt?"},

{"day":7,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 31-35","title":"Wrestling Toward a New Name",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 31","Jacob flees from Laban's growing hostility, and the two eventually make an uneasy covenant of peace."),
  ("Gen 32","Jacob wrestles all night with a mysterious divine figure at Peniel and emerges renamed 'Israel,' walking with a permanent limp."),
  ("Gen 33","Jacob anxiously prepares to face Esau, whose surprisingly gracious reception undercuts years of dreaded confrontation."),
  ("Gen 34","Dinah's assault by Shechem leads to a violent, disproportionate revenge by her brothers Simeon and Levi."),
  ("Gen 35","God renews His covenant with Jacob at Bethel; Rachel dies giving birth to Benjamin, and Isaac dies soon afterward."),
 ],
 "themes":["Real transformation sometimes comes through a costly, wrestling encounter with God, not an easy fix.","Reconciliation can arrive more graciously than our fears anticipate."],
 "expositor":("ferguson","Ferguson has taught that Jacob's wrestling match at Peniel pictures how God sometimes must bring us to the end of our own self-reliant scheming before we're ready to receive a new identity and a new dependence on Him."),
 "reflect":"Is there a 'wrestling' season in your own life that might actually be God working to reshape your identity, not just an obstacle to get past?"},

{"day":8,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 36-40","title":"From the Pit to Potiphar's House",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 36","Esau's genealogy traces the origins of the Edomite nation, Israel's later persistent rival."),
  ("Gen 37","Joseph's brothers, resentful of his dreams and favored status, sell him into Egyptian slavery."),
  ("Gen 38","Judah's shameful dealings with his daughter-in-law Tamar interrupt the Joseph story, exposing his own moral failure even as God's grace works through it."),
  ("Gen 39","Joseph resists Potiphar's wife's seduction with integrity yet is unjustly imprisoned -- while the text repeatedly notes 'the LORD was with Joseph.'"),
  ("Gen 40","While imprisoned, Joseph interprets the dreams of Pharaoh's cupbearer and baker, setting up his eventual rise to power."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's presence and purpose can remain active even in seasons of injustice and setback.","Integrity under pressure often looks costly before it looks rewarded."],
 "expositor":("waltke","Waltke pointed out that Genesis 39's repeated refrain 'the LORD was with Joseph' -- both in Potiphar's house and in prison -- teaches that God's presence, not circumstance, is the real measure of a person's true standing."),
 "reflect":"In a current season that feels unjust or stuck, what would it look like to trust that God's presence with you doesn't depend on your circumstances improving first?"},

{"day":9,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 41-45","title":"From Prison to the Palace, Then Reconciliation",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 41","Joseph interprets Pharaoh's dreams of coming famine and is elevated to second-in-command over all of Egypt."),
  ("Gen 42","Joseph's brothers come to Egypt seeking grain, not recognizing him, and he tests their character without revealing his identity."),
  ("Gen 43","The brothers return with Benjamin as required, and Joseph's emotions nearly overwhelm him as reconciliation draws closer."),
  ("Gen 44","Joseph's final test -- a planted silver cup -- reveals Judah's transformed character as he offers himself in Benjamin's place."),
  ("Gen 45","Joseph reveals his identity to his stunned brothers, forgiving them and recognizing God's sovereign hand even in their evil intentions."),
 ],
 "themes":["God can work through even betrayal and injustice to accomplish good purposes.","Genuine forgiveness sees God's larger sovereignty behind another person's wrongdoing."],
 "expositor":("davis","Dale Ralph Davis observed that Judah's offer to take Benjamin's place in chapter 44 marks a stunning transformation from the man who once sold his own brother into slavery -- proof that God's grace can genuinely remake even deeply flawed character over time."),
 "reflect":"Is there a wrong done against you where recognizing God's larger purpose, as Joseph did, might help you move toward forgiveness?"},

{"day":10,"book":"Genesis","ref":"Genesis 46-50","title":"You Intended Harm; God Intended Good",
 "chapters":[
  ("Gen 46","Jacob's entire family relocates to Egypt, fulfilling God's earlier promise to Abraham that his descendants would multiply in a foreign land."),
  ("Gen 47","Joseph settles his family in the fertile region of Goshen and skillfully manages Egypt's famine relief."),
  ("Gen 48","Jacob blesses Joseph's sons Ephraim and Manasseh, deliberately crossing his hands to bless the younger over the elder."),
  ("Gen 49","Jacob's final prophetic blessings over his twelve sons foreshadow the tribes' futures, especially Judah's coming royal line."),
  ("Gen 50","Jacob dies and is buried in Canaan; Joseph forgives his brothers with the defining statement, 'You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good.'"),
 ],
 "themes":["God's sovereign purposes can incorporate, without excusing, human sin and evil intentions.","Genesis closes still looking forward to the land and offspring God originally promised Abraham."],
 "expositor":("sailhamer","Sailhamer noted that Genesis 50:20 -- 'you intended to harm me, but God intended it for good' -- functions as a lens for reading the entire book of Genesis, where human failure repeatedly becomes the very material God weaves into His unfolding covenant plan."),
 "reflect":"Is there a painful chapter of your own story where you can begin to see, even partially, how God has intended good where others intended harm?"},

{"day":11,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 1-5","title":"Oppression, a Burning Bush, and a Reluctant Deliverer",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 1","Israel multiplies rapidly in Egypt, prompting Pharaoh's escalating oppression, including a policy of infanticide against Hebrew baby boys."),
  ("Ex 2","Moses is born, hidden, and saved, raised in Pharaoh's own household, then flees to Midian after killing an Egyptian overseer."),
  ("Ex 3","God appears to Moses in a burning bush, revealing His covenant name -- 'I AM WHO I AM' -- and commissioning him to deliver Israel."),
  ("Ex 4","God equips the reluctant Moses with miraculous signs and appoints Aaron as his spokesman."),
  ("Ex 5","Moses' initial request to Pharaoh backfires, increasing Israel's suffering and testing the people's trust in God's unfolding plan."),
 ],
 "themes":["God notices and responds to His people's suffering, even during long silence.","Reluctance and inadequacy don't disqualify someone from God's call."],
 "expositor":("kaiser","Kaiser has emphasized that God's covenant name revealed at the burning bush -- 'I AM' -- announces His self-existent, ever-present faithfulness, precisely the assurance Moses (and Israel) needed heading into an apparently impossible confrontation with the world's superpower."),
 "reflect":"Like Moses, is there a task you feel inadequate for that God might still be calling you toward anyway?"},

{"day":12,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 6-10","title":"Confronting Pharaoh's Gods",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 6","God reaffirms His covenant name and promise to Moses despite the initial setback with Pharaoh."),
  ("Ex 7","The plagues begin as Aaron's staff becomes a snake and the Nile River turns to blood."),
  ("Ex 8","The plagues of frogs, gnats, and flies press Pharaoh, who repeatedly relents under pressure and then hardens his heart again."),
  ("Ex 9","The plagues of livestock death, boils, and hail intensify, each one arguably confronting a specific Egyptian deity's claimed domain."),
  ("Ex 10","The plagues of locusts and darkness bring Egypt near total collapse, yet Pharaoh still refuses to fully release Israel."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's judgments directly confronted and exposed the powerlessness of Egypt's false gods.","A hardened heart can persist even through repeated, undeniable displays of God's power."],
 "expositor":("mcgee","McGee often taught that each Egyptian plague targeted a specific deity Egypt worshiped -- the Nile-god, the frog-goddess, the sun-god -- turning the plagues into a systematic, public dismantling of Egypt's entire religious system, not random calamities."),
 "reflect":"Is there an area of stubborn resistance in your own life that has persisted despite repeated, clear evidence you should change course?"},

{"day":13,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 11-15","title":"Passover and Deliverance",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 11","The tenth and final plague -- the death of every Egyptian firstborn -- is announced."),
  ("Ex 12","The first Passover is instituted, with the blood of a lamb sparing Israel's homes, and the nation is finally released after 430 years of slavery."),
  ("Ex 13","Israel consecrates the firstborn and begins the exodus journey, guided visibly by God's pillar of cloud and fire."),
  ("Ex 14","Pharaoh's army pursues Israel to the Red Sea, which God miraculously parts, delivering Israel and destroying Egypt's forces."),
  ("Ex 15","Moses and Miriam lead Israel in triumphant song after their deliverance, before facing their first wilderness test of trust at Marah."),
 ],
 "themes":["Deliverance came only through a substitute's blood, foreshadowing the ultimate Passover Lamb.","Even dramatic deliverance doesn't immediately erase the need for ongoing trust."],
 "expositor":("kidner","Kidner noted that the Passover lamb's blood, applied to the doorframes so death would 'pass over,' establishes a pattern of substitutionary rescue that the New Testament directly identifies with Christ, 'our Passover lamb' (1 Corinthians 5:7)."),
 "reflect":"Israel sang triumphantly at the Red Sea, then complained days later at Marah. Where have you seen a similar gap between celebrating God's past deliverance and trusting Him with a present difficulty?"},

{"day":14,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 16-20","title":"Provision, Guidance, and the Ten Words",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 16","God provides manna and quail in the wilderness, teaching Israel to depend on His provision one day at a time."),
  ("Ex 17","God provides water from a rock, and Israel defeats the attacking Amalekites as Moses' raised hands, supported by Aaron and Hur, secure the victory."),
  ("Ex 18","Moses' father-in-law Jethro wisely advises him to delegate leadership, establishing a structure of appointed judges."),
  ("Ex 19","Israel arrives at Mount Sinai, and God's terrifying, holy presence descends on the mountain to prepare the people for covenant-making."),
  ("Ex 20","God gives the Ten Commandments, the moral foundation of Israel's covenant relationship with Him."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's provision is often given daily, not all at once, to build ongoing trust.","The Ten Commandments describe the shape of a covenant relationship, not merely abstract rules."],
 "expositor":("sailhamer","Sailhamer emphasized that the manna's daily provision (with no ability to hoard it, except before the Sabbath) was designed to train Israel's dependence on God's ongoing care rather than self-sufficient stockpiling."),
 "reflect":"Is there an area where you're tempted to 'hoard' security rather than trust God's provision one day at a time, the way Israel had to with manna?"},

{"day":15,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 21-25","title":"Justice for Everyday Life, and a Dwelling Place",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 21","Case laws regarding servitude and personal injury apply the Ten Commandments' principles to everyday justice."),
  ("Ex 22","Further case laws address property disputes, social responsibility, and care for the vulnerable, including widows, orphans, and foreigners."),
  ("Ex 23","Laws promote impartial justice, Sabbath rest for the land, and three annual festivals, alongside God's promise to send an angel ahead into Canaan."),
  ("Ex 24","Israel formally ratifies the covenant with sacrificial blood, and Moses ascends the mountain into God's glory."),
  ("Ex 25","God gives detailed instructions for the Tabernacle's Ark, table, and lampstand -- His intended dwelling place among His people."),
 ],
 "themes":["God's law consistently connects worship of Him with practical justice toward the vulnerable.","God desired to dwell physically among His people, not remain distant."],
 "expositor":("ferguson","Ferguson has noted that the case laws following the Ten Commandments show that biblical holiness was never meant to stay abstract -- it was meant to reshape how Israel treated servants, neighbors, and the poor in very concrete, everyday situations."),
 "reflect":"Which of these case laws' underlying concern -- justice for the vulnerable -- shows up as a practical opportunity in your own life this week?"},

{"day":16,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 26-30","title":"Designing God's Dwelling Place",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 26","Instructions for the Tabernacle's structure -- curtains, frames, and the veil separating the Most Holy Place -- emphasize both beauty and separation."),
  ("Ex 27","Instructions for the bronze altar and courtyard, along with provision for keeping the lampstand continually burning."),
  ("Ex 28","Detailed instructions for the priestly garments, especially the high priest's ornate breastpiece bearing the names of Israel's twelve tribes over his heart."),
  ("Ex 29","Instructions for consecrating Aaron and his sons as priests through detailed sacrificial ordination ceremonies."),
  ("Ex 30","Instructions for the incense altar, atonement money, bronze basin, and the specially formulated anointing oil and incense."),
 ],
 "themes":["Access to God's presence required careful, God-specified mediation, not casual approach.","Every detail of Tabernacle design pointed toward the seriousness and beauty of approaching a holy God."],
 "expositor":("kidner","Kidner observed that the high priest carrying Israel's twelve tribes 'over his heart' on the breastpiece pictured a mediator who represented the people before God -- a role the New Testament shows fulfilled completely and permanently in Christ."),
 "reflect":"How does the elaborate care given to approaching God in the Tabernacle shape your own sense of the privilege of approaching Him today through Christ?"},

{"day":17,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 31-35","title":"The Golden Calf and God's Enduring Patience",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 31","God appoints Spirit-gifted craftsmen for the Tabernacle's construction and reaffirms the Sabbath as an enduring covenant sign."),
  ("Ex 32","While Moses is on the mountain, Israel commits catastrophic idolatry with the golden calf, provoking God's righteous anger and Moses' urgent intercession."),
  ("Ex 33","Moses pleads for God's continued presence with Israel despite their sin, and is granted a partial glimpse of God's glory."),
  ("Ex 34","The covenant and stone tablets are renewed after Moses' intercession, and his face shines from having been in God's presence."),
  ("Ex 35","Israel willingly and generously brings offerings for the Tabernacle's construction, and the Spirit-gifted craftsmen begin the work."),
 ],
 "themes":["Even catastrophic covenant unfaithfulness did not end God's commitment to His people.","Bold intercession on behalf of others reflects the heart of a true mediator."],
 "expositor":("davis","Dale Ralph Davis has pointed out that Moses' intercession in Exodus 32-34 -- pleading for Israel's forgiveness and God's continued presence rather than abandoning them -- models the kind of bold, others-focused prayer every believer is invited to imitate."),
 "reflect":"Is there a person or situation you could intercede for more boldly, following Moses' example, rather than simply observing from a distance?"},

{"day":18,"book":"Exodus","ref":"Exodus 36-40","title":"God's Glory Fills the Tabernacle",
 "chapters":[
  ("Ex 36","The people's generosity actually exceeds what's needed for the Tabernacle's construction, so Moses has to restrain further giving."),
  ("Ex 37","The Ark of the Covenant, the table, and the lampstand are constructed exactly as God had instructed."),
  ("Ex 38","The bronze altar, basin, and courtyard are completed, with the total materials used carefully documented."),
  ("Ex 39","The priestly garments are finished precisely according to God's specifications, and Moses inspects and blesses the completed work."),
  ("Ex 40","The Tabernacle is assembled and consecrated, and God's glory fills it so powerfully that even Moses cannot enter -- Exodus closes with God dwelling visibly among His people."),
 ],
 "themes":["Careful, obedient attention to detail in worship matters to God.","Exodus's story arc -- from slavery to God's dwelling presence -- is the whole book's climax and goal."],
 "expositor":("sailhamer","Sailhamer noted that Exodus deliberately ends not with Israel's freedom alone but with God's glory filling the Tabernacle -- showing that the entire exodus story was always aimed at God dwelling with His people, not merely their escape from Egypt."),
 "reflect":"Exodus's whole story moves toward God's presence dwelling with His people. How central is pursuing God's presence, versus merely His blessings, in your own faith right now?"},
]
